<p>U.S. and Iranian negotiators remained deadlocked over control of the Strait of Hormuz, even as some oil tankers resumed moving through the waterway and the U.S. military said two destroyers had entered the strait for mine-clearing operations. Talks in Islamabad focused on Washington’s demand that Iran reopen the route immediately to all maritime traffic. Iranian officials said they would do so only after a final peace deal. Those mixed signals captured both the danger and a narrow opening for progress. Shipping data showed three supertankers leaving the Gulf through Hormuz as negotiations began, suggesting that at least some commercial traffic could still move. But other vessels reportedly turned back after the talks faltered, and Tehran rejected U.S. claims about the naval transit, a reminder of how fragile the ceasefire and any broader de-escalation remain in one of the world’s most important energy chokepoints.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/xBaYkHNXSdK84gB6V0nclrG6Scm_5ZuBVf3oSj7RW1T2PPtV2kbNuGxehuB7vc2Ol7ru1QEC_wGzxAt9Ge_ebQeg_aOgYPL1ttJqpmVxkJ_l4R0LiIWB_oXzlrbrqkI6C4sHWz3qmazM413W-TM12MsK394iZlqaFjlw4D3oIsG7r-M205YXdhUIYNcUmDu1OF5qzGtAzpo2snuNe75qXS4sJYh62mTx9a77Vz2gpmjOQQ' alt='Journalists gather in Islamabad, Pakistan, to cover talks between U.S. and Iranian officials.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Safe passage: CENTCOM commander Admiral Brad Cooper said the U.S. had started establishing a new maritime passage and would soon share it with the shipping industry as mine-clearing continued.</li><li>Energy rerouting: President Trump said empty tankers were heading to the United States to load oil and gas, casting U.S. supply as an alternative while Hormuz traffic remains disrupted.</li><li>Iranian warning: After disputing the U.S. account of the naval transit, Iran’s Revolutionary Guards threatened to deal “severely” with military vessels crossing the waterway, underscoring how quickly the crisis could flare again.</li><li>Voyage uncertainty: The reasons behind the supertanker U-turns were unclear, showing that ship operators were responding to fast-moving risks even without a formal explanation.</li></ul><blockquote>Today, we began the process of establishing a new passage and we will share this safe pathway with the maritime industry soon to encourage the free flow of commerce - Admiral Brad Cooper</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>United States: Washington said two Navy destroyers transited the strait to begin mine-clearing, presenting the operation as a step toward restoring commercial shipping. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/strait-of-hormuz-naval-destroyers-cross-centcom-iran-mines/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Iran: Iranian officials rejected the U.S. account of the transit and said the strait should reopen only as part of a final peace agreement, not as an immediate concession. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/us-warships-transit-strait-of-hormuz-in-mine-clearance-operation/article70852232.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>President Trump: The president said the United States had started &#x27;clearing out&#x27; the strait and described the effort as helping countries including China, Japan and France that depend on the route. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1990850/us-warships-transit-strait-of-hormuz-in-mine-clearance-operation-centcom'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Negotiators: Officials involved in the talks described them as stalled, with access to Hormuz emerging as one of the central unresolved issues. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/d5c00c1b-0807-4f94-b181-4f3a50937540'>Financial Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/4/12/oil-tankers-exit-strait-of-hormuz-amid-fragile-us-iran-ceasefire'>Oil tankers exit Strait of Hormuz amid fragile US-Iran ceasefire</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/two-supertankers-u-turn-in-hormuz-as-us-iran-talks-break-down'>Two supertankers ma